<%# locals: managed_app, status %>
<%# An app whose config cannot be parsed has no service/destination/primary_host/accessories to use —
    the panel would raise Kamal::ConfigParser::ParseError (status is nil exactly then; locks/show checks
    last_poll_error the same way). The cost is that in the short window between a parse failure and the next
    poll the action buttons go away along with the data — the width of this window depends on the fastest
    polling interval in PollCadence (currently BURST = 2 seconds), and is not a constant that always holds:
    if the polling interval is widened later, this window will quietly widen with it, and at that point it
    must be re-evaluated whether this is still enough (a minor from the task-5 review). %>
<%# Reading the lock really has to SSH in, and one unreachable host makes it wait out the full connection
    timeout (measured 3.4 seconds), while everything else here comes from the database and the config. So the
    buttons render right away with the lock "being read", and the frame then reloads the same panel with the
    lock state it read. Not loading: :lazy — lazy would wait until the frame scrolls into view, moving the
    slowness to the moment of scrolling. target _top: the forms inside lead to the action page, which must
    replace the whole page, not this frame. %>
<section class="section actions">
  <h2><%= t("managed_apps.show.actions") %></h2>
  <% if managed_app.last_poll_error.present? %>
    <p class="hint"><%= t("managed_apps.actions_panel.parse_error") %></p>
  <% else %>
    <% lock = lock_state_for(managed_app) %>
    <%# Without an SSH key there is no lock to read: render the final state and skip the request. %>
    <%= turbo_frame_tag :lock_and_actions, src: (managed_app_lock_path(managed_app) unless lock[:state] == :no_key),
                        target: "_top" do %>
      <%= render "managed_apps/actions_panel", managed_app: managed_app, lock: lock %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</section>
